※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4205

※ The body of a generic function is checked for each set of types a call gives
※ it: what may be done to a value of a type parameter is what may be done to
※ the type it turned out to be, and nothing before the call knows what that is.
※
※ So the message points at the line the mistake is written on, in the
※ definition, and a note says which call asked for those types -- the one thing
※ a reader cannot see from there.  That is C++'s bargain, and it is what this
※ language can promise without a language for constraints.

fn largest(a: T', b: T') → T':
    a ⌈ b

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let s: ⸨u8⸩ = ⸨1u8⸩
    let t: ⸨u8⸩ = ⸨2u8⸩
    let n: ⸨u8⸩ = largest(s, t)
    if #n = 2:
        0u6
    else:
        1u6
